studio: the lens view, fm, and the terminals
  Each line is a slot, the rest of the line what runs in it, and the
  lines under a slot its tabs, which take turns in it, the first shown.
  The first slot that names nothing keeps a shell, the terminals, and is
  the panel, across the frame's bottom, 38% of it high; the rest take
  the room above it in the order they come, a golden spiral: the first
  62% of it, at the right, and each after it 62% of what is left, down
  and across in turn, the last what remains (design/lens). The lens view
  shows the thing at fm's cursor: its source in ed -f, where it is typed,
  and its outline, its render and its diff, each the lens's, from the
  text as ed holds it. fm -f and ed -f follow cur, so ed edits whatever
  fm's cursor is on, and both go where a click anywhere says. A slot's
  or a tab's name is how a program reaches it: studio send shell make
  pastes make into the shell, and studio read shell reads it. fm's slot
  is the frame's face: its q ends the frame, asking first while ed holds
  work unsaved. The keys are in studio/cheatsheet.
view
  source    ed -f
  outline   studio show outline
  render    studio show render
  diff      studio show diff
fm          fm -f
